\par\pard\plain \sa180\sb180\ltrch\loch {It is not often that the Edenfield Press undertakes a project of such profound historical significance as Professor Edward Welton\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s }{\f0\fs24\i1 The History of Man and Elves}{. The manuscript first proposed to us was, to put it plainly, an audacious one: to translate, contextualize, and publish the contents of a library whose existence had been, until recently, a matter of myth. I am referring, of course, to the personal library of the Elf Catherine, discovered in the remote Eastern Dunes following her tragic death during the Siege of Edenfield.}
\par\plain {The purpose of this work, and the subsequent volumes that shall follow, is to distill the vast corpus of knowledge contained within Catherine\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s writings. Professor Welton has argued\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 persuasively and relentlessly, I might add\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 since he was still completing his doctoral thesis that this material holds the key to filling the great lacunae in our understanding of the Elder Days. He contended that it would shed a new, unbiased light upon a history thus far told only by its victors. After careful consideration of the preliminary findings, the Edenfield University Press agreed to lend its full support to this monumental endeavor.}
\par\plain {On a personal note, my acquaintance with the author precedes his professorship. I first knew him as a first-year student at the University, a young man who went by "Eddie" to a close-knit and notoriously energetic band of friends. It is a matter of university record that his boundless curiosity was matched only by his talent for discovering the precise limits of campus regulations. Indeed, it is a testament to his unique ingenuity that the Office of Student Conduct was required to draft three new addendums to its code of conduct in his first year alone.}
\par\plain {During that period, in my former capacity as a Scholarship Agent for the university, I had the opportunity to review his file. The funding for his education was provided by a private benefactor of immense wealth and considerable eccentricity, listed simply as 'C. Bolos.' It was only years later that I would learn this was the preferred public alias of Catherine the Elf herself. One does not, I believe, question the judgment of a three-thousand-year-old Elf when it comes to selecting a prot\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u233\'E9g\u233\'E9. His integrity, therefore, has the rarest and most unimpeachable of endorsements.}
\par\plain {This volume is but the first of Professor Welton's efforts to dissect the vast archive Catherine left to posterity. In an age where the subtle arts are waning, and the world\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s youth seems more interested in the logic of steam-gears and electrical currents, some may question the relevance of such a work. Professor Welton maintains that the translation of these texts from Old Elvish into the common tongue is not an exercise in nostalgia. It is, instead, a vital act of preservation, ensuring that future generations of students\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 should they choose to walk the path of magic\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 will do so with a foundation of understanding far more complete than any before them.}
\par\plain {The Edenfield Press is proud to present his findings.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b1 Emma Somers}{ Head Editor, Edenfield Press University of Edenfield August, 2000}}